# The container that leaves root-owned files in a developer's checkout (2026-10-04)

`scripts/verify-in-docker.sh` runs its gates in a container with the working tree
bind-mounted at `/src`. The container runs as root. Anything it writes through that mount
therefore belongs to root *inside the developer's checkout*, which is a different thing from
writing into its own filesystem: the developer cannot delete it, and the next person's
`git clean -fdx` does not remove what git can see either.

Two places this happened, one fix in two files, and `scripts/ci/check-container-hygiene.py`
to keep it from coming back. Output below is quoted verbatim, including its own punctuation.

== 1. What one gate run left behind, as shipped

The entry point at `11cdd8a2` passed `RUST_MIN_STACK` and the git-ownership variables to the
container and nothing that stops a Python cache. Sixteen of the 29 test modules under
`scripts/ci` load their gate module with `importlib.util.spec_from_file_location` instead of
running it as a subprocess, and a load through the mount writes a cache. The other thirteen
shell the gate out and write nothing. With `scripts/ci/__pycache__` cleared of container-owned
files first:

$ echo "root-owned before run: $(find . -path ./target -prune -o -user root -print | wc -l)"
    root-owned before run: 0
$ IMAGE_TAG=chaos-verify:frozen ./scripts/verify-in-docker.sh --only "docs path references"
    check-doc-path-refs: 275 Markdown documents scanned against 4011 tracked paths, 48 dangling mention(s), 26 recorded; every link resolves and every dangling mention is recorded and live

    selected gates passed in chaos-verify:frozen
      --only was in effect: 1 of 33 gates selected, this is not a sweep
$ find . -path ./target -prune -o -user root -print
    ./scripts/ci/__pycache__/check-doc-path-refs.cpython-311.pyc
$ echo "root-owned after run: $(find . -path ./target -prune -o -user root -print | wc -l)"
    root-owned after run: 1

The `--full` run that morning left two such files, `check-timeout-child.cpython-311.pyc` and
`test-installer-asset-names.cpython-311.pyc`, timestamps 12:16 and 12:24. They are `311`
caches because the container is Debian bookworm; the host runs 3.10, so nothing on the host
ever reads them and nothing on the host ever rewrites them.

This one cannot be re-run at the head of this branch, because the fix is in place. Section 2
has a command that reproduces the same behaviour on demand.

== 2. Why a cache directory is the damage, and a cache file mostly is not

A file the container writes inside a directory the developer created can still be unlinked,
because unlinking needs write permission on the directory:

$ rm scripts/ci/__pycache__/check-doc-path-refs.cpython-311.pyc; echo "exit=$?"
    exit=0

What cannot be undone is the directory the container *creates*. Where no cache directory
exists yet, the container makes one, owned by root, and from then on its files cannot be
removed by the owner of the tree:

$ d="$(mktemp -d)" && printf 'X = 1\n' > "$d/probe_mod.py"
$ docker run --rm -e PYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE=0 -v "$d:/src" --workdir /src chaos-verify:frozen python3 -c 'import probe_mod; print("imported", probe_mod.X)'
    imported 1
$ ls -ld "$d" "$d/__pycache__" "$d/__pycache__"/*.pyc
    drwx------ 3 chaos chaos 4096 Oct  4 13:29 /tmp/tmp.VzDGjtnoXB
    drwxr-xr-x 2 root  root  4096 Oct  4 13:29 /tmp/tmp.VzDGjtnoXB/__pycache__
    -rw-r----- 1 root  root   134 Oct  4 13:29 /tmp/tmp.VzDGjtnoXB/__pycache__/probe_mod.cpython-311.pyc
$ rm -rf "$d"; echo "exit=$?"
    rm: cannot remove '/tmp/tmp.VzDGjtnoXB/__pycache__/probe_mod.cpython-311.pyc': Permission denied
    exit=1

`PYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE=0` is the opt-out, so this command asks for exactly the behaviour
the unfixed entry point had, from the image as it is now. This is what deleting the frozen
clone used by the `--full` run hit, fourteen lines of it:

    rm: cannot remove '/var/tmp/verify-frozen-a04e91a7/scripts/ci/__pycache__/panic-site-census.cpython-311.pyc': Permission denied
    rm: cannot remove '/var/tmp/verify-frozen-a04e91a7/scripts/ci/__pycache__/check-protocol-mirror.cpython-311.pyc': Permission denied
    rm: cannot remove '/var/tmp/verify-frozen-a04e91a7/scripts/ci/__pycache__/check-evidence-paths.cpython-311.pyc': Permission denied

That clone was a throwaway, so a privileged `rm -rf` finished the job. A developer's checkout
is not, and the same directory appearing under `scripts/` or `crates/` is permanent until
somebody notices and reaches for `sudo`.

== 3. The same shape, one size bigger: the build directory

The entry point mounts a named volume over `/src/target` for exactly this reason. Without
that line, the cargo output would be written into the checkout as root, and the first thing
written there is the directory itself:

$ docker run --rm -v "$PWD:/src" --workdir /src chaos-verify:frozen bash -c 'mkdir -p /src/target && touch /src/target/root-owned-probe && ls -l /src/target/root-owned-probe'
    -rw-r--r-- 1 root root 0 Oct  4 05:23 /src/target/root-owned-probe
$ ls -l target/root-owned-probe
    -rw-r--r-- 1 root root 0 Oct  4 13:23 target/root-owned-probe

The build directory on this workspace is around 40 GB, which is the other reason the check
treats a missing `/src/target` volume as a finding rather than a style point.

The volume keeps the build output out of the checkout. It does not keep the directory, and
this one was only found after the two-place fix had been written: a mount point that the
image does not already have is created by the runtime *inside the parent mount*, and the
parent here is the bind-mounted checkout. A clean clone of `11cdd8a2`, one gate, came back
holding exactly one root-owned path and nothing else:

$ cd /var/tmp/verify-frozen-clean && git rev-parse --short HEAD
    11cdd8a2
$ IMAGE_TAG=chaos-verify:frozen ./scripts/verify-in-docker.sh --only "pipefail report" >/dev/null 2>&1
$ find . -user root
    ./target
$ ls -ld target; ls -A target | wc -l
    drwxr-xr-x 2 root root 4096 Oct  4 13:36 target
    0

Being empty, that directory is still removable, because removing it needs write permission
on the parent and the parent belongs to the developer. It is the untidy part, not the
undeletable part:

$ rmdir target; echo "exit=$?"
    exit=0

Creating it on the host before the container starts takes the creation away from the
runtime, and after that a run leaves no root-owned path anywhere in the tree:

$ mkdir -p target && ls -ld target
    drwxrwx--- 2 chaos chaos 4096 Oct  4 13:41 target
$ IMAGE_TAG=chaos-verify:frozen ./scripts/verify-in-docker.sh --only "pipefail report" >/dev/null 2>&1
$ find . -user root | wc -l
    0
$ ls -ld target
    drwxrwx--- 2 chaos chaos 4096 Oct  4 13:41 target

The two runs above are the same command against the same commit; the only difference is who
had created `/src/target` first.

== 4. The fix, which is in three places

`docker/verify.Dockerfile` gains the image-level default, next to the one it already set:

    # Test-thread stack for the large xai-grok-shell actor tests, same as CI.
    ENV RUST_MIN_STACK=16777216

    # The repository is bind-mounted at /src and the guards in scripts/ci import each
    # other through that mount. Bytecode caches created there belong to root and land in
    # the developer's checkout: a directory the container created cannot be removed by a
    # plain `rm -rf`, only by a privileged one.
    ENV PYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE=1

`scripts/verify-in-docker.sh` passes it too, in `run_args`, because `IMAGE_TAG` may point at
an image built elsewhere and the guarantee should not depend on which image was used:

    --env RUST_MIN_STACK=16777216
    # The image sets this as well; it is repeated here so an IMAGE_TAG built elsewhere
    # cannot reintroduce the leak. The guards in scripts/ci import each other through the
    # /src bind mount, and a __pycache__ directory the container creates there belongs to
    # root and survives the developer's own `rm -rf`.
    --env PYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE=1

and it creates the build directory on the host before the container is started, which is
what the section 3 measurement asked for:

    docker volume create chaos-verify-target >/dev/null
    # The runtime creates a mount point the image does not already have, and it creates it as
    # root inside whatever is mounted at its parent -- so `--volume chaos-verify-target:/src/target`
    # alone leaves a root-owned `target/` in the checkout even though nothing is ever written
    # through it. One gate run on 2026-10-04 did exactly that. Created here first, the directory
    # belongs to whoever ran the gates, and the run then leaves no root-owned path at all.
    mkdir -p "${repo_root}/target"

Both `ENV` lines are placed after the last `RUN`, so rebuilding the image replays nothing
expensive: the layers that follow `ENV` are `WORKDIR` and `CMD`.

== 5. After the fix, the same three measurements

The entry point again, same gate, same tree:

$ IMAGE_TAG=chaos-verify:frozen ./scripts/verify-in-docker.sh --only "docs path references" 2>&1 | tail -3
    selected gates passed in chaos-verify:frozen
      --only was in effect: 1 of 33 gates selected, this is not a sweep
$ echo "root-owned after: $(find . -path ./target -prune -o -user root -print | wc -l)"
    root-owned after: 0

The image default on its own, without the entry point in between:

$ docker run --rm chaos-verify:frozen printenv PYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE
    1
$ e="$(mktemp -d)" && printf 'X = 1\n' > "$e/probe_mod.py"
$ docker run --rm -v "$e:/src" --workdir /src chaos-verify:frozen python3 -c 'import probe_mod; print("imported", probe_mod.X)'
    imported 1
$ echo "mount contents: $(ls -A "$e" | tr '\n' ' ')"
    mount contents: probe_mod.py
$ rm -rf "$e"; echo "exit=$?"
    exit=0

The mount comes back containing only what the host put there, so it deletes normally.

== 6. The check

`scripts/ci/check-container-hygiene.py` reads every `scripts/*.sh` and every
`docker/*.Dockerfile`. Which script is judged is decided by what it mounts, not by its name:
a mount whose host side names the checkout (`repo_root`, `repo_dir`, `checkout`,
`source_root`) is what triggers the rules. Four rules:

* a script that mounts the checkout has to pass `PYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE=1`;
* `<mount>/target` has to be covered by a named volume, or `CARGO_TARGET_DIR` has to point
  outside the mount -- pointing it at `/src/build` is not an escape;
* where that volume is what keeps the build output out, the script has to create the host side
  of it first, so the runtime is never the one making the mount point inside the checkout. A
  `mkdir` inside a gate command is not accepted: it runs on the container side, which is the
  side this check exists to distrust;
* the image has to set the same variable in an `ENV`, so a bare `docker run` and the
  `--shell` this entry point offers behave like the gates do.

Comments do not count in either language, and `ENV NAME VALUE` without the equals sign is
accepted because it is the other valid Dockerfile spelling.

$ python3 scripts/ci/check-container-hygiene.py --verbose
    judged scripts/collect-wsl-info.sh
    judged scripts/install-hooks.sh
    judged scripts/install-integrity-in-docker.sh
    judged scripts/install-integrity-powershell.sh
    judged scripts/install-sh-in-docker.sh
    judged scripts/install.sh
    judged scripts/l10n-guard.sh
    judged scripts/npm-install-in-docker.sh
    judged scripts/remote-acceptance-in-docker.sh
    judged scripts/test-blitz-stress.sh
    judged scripts/test-platform.sh
    judged scripts/upstream-recon.sh
    judged scripts/verify-gates.sh
    judged scripts/verify-in-docker.sh
    judged scripts/verify-release-signature.sh
    judged scripts/web-deployment-in-docker.sh
    judged docker/verify.Dockerfile
    container-hygiene: OK (16 shell script(s) scanned, 1 mounts the checkout, 1 Dockerfile(s), 0 problem(s))

One of the sixteen mounts the checkout. The other five `*-in-docker.sh` entry points mount a
lab directory (`${lab_root}/host:/lab` and friends) and are not judged for it, which is why
the rule keys on the mount: renaming a script must not change who is responsible.

== 7. The check against the files as shipped

Pinned to `11cdd8a2`, the commit before this change, so the comparison does not drift:

$ t="$(mktemp -d)" && mkdir -p "$t/scripts" "$t/docker"
$ git show "11cdd8a2:scripts/verify-in-docker.sh" > "$t/scripts/verify-in-docker.sh"
$ git show "11cdd8a2:docker/verify.Dockerfile" > "$t/docker/verify.Dockerfile"
$ python3 scripts/ci/check-container-hygiene.py --root "$t"; echo "exit=$?"
    /tmp/tmp.PxQT0rRxWp/scripts/verify-in-docker.sh: mounts the checkout at /src but PYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE is never set; the guards in scripts/ci import each other through that mount, and the bytecode caches they write belong to root inside the checkout
    /tmp/tmp.PxQT0rRxWp/scripts/verify-in-docker.sh: /src/target is a named volume mounted inside the checkout but the script never creates ${repo_root}/target on the host, so the runtime makes that mount point as root and the checkout ends up holding a directory its owner did not create
    /tmp/tmp.PxQT0rRxWp/docker/verify.Dockerfile: PYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE is never set in the image; a bare `docker run` with this tree mounted, including the `--shell` the entry point offers, writes caches as root regardless of what the entry point passes
    container-hygiene: 3 problem(s) in 1 shell script(s) scanned, 1 mounting the checkout, 1 Dockerfile(s)
    exit=1

Three findings, and the middle one is the section 3 defect: `/src/target` was covered by a
volume at `11cdd8a2`, so the build output stayed out of the tree, but nothing on the host
had created the directory the volume is mounted on:

$ grep -c 'mkdir' "$t/scripts/verify-in-docker.sh"
    0

== 8. Fixtures, and what happens when each decision is undone

`scripts/ci/test-check-container-hygiene.py` builds a throwaway tree and drives the check as
a subprocess. 19 cases, all green on the shipped check:

$ python3 scripts/ci/test-check-container-hygiene.py 2>&1 | tail -3
    Ran 19 tests in 0.507s

    OK

Seven of them are "must not report": the shipped pair (runner plus image), the three `--env`
spellings the shell accepts, a named volume over `/src/target` paired with the host-side
`mkdir`, a `CARGO_TARGET_DIR` outside the mount (which needs no `mkdir`, because nothing is
mounted inside the checkout then), `ENV NAME VALUE` in the Dockerfile, the lab runner that
mounts nothing of the checkout, and the repository itself. That last one also asserts the
scan reached at least a dozen scripts, which is what keeps a check that scanned nothing from
passing. Two more are the shipped text before the fix -- the `run_args` array and the
Dockerfile tail copied out of `11cdd8a2` -- so the shape that was actually running stays a
test case. Three cases are the mount point: without the host `mkdir`, with a `mkdir` only
inside a gate command, and with a host `mkdir` of some other directory.

Eighteen mutations of the check, one deliberate revert each, applied to the shipped file,
the fixture suite run against it, the file restored with a plain `cp` and verified with `cmp`.
As in the other batteries here, the unmutated baseline runs first and a non-green baseline
aborts, the verdict counts unittest's `FAIL:` and `ERROR:` lines so a mutation that breaks
the file so badly that no test runs is printed as SURVIVED rather than as a kill, and an
anchor that does not appear exactly once is not applied and is likewise reported as SURVIVED.

    baseline (unmutated gate): 'OK', failures=0

    KILLED  4  M1 bytecode rule dropped entirely
    KILLED  1  M2 any value accepted, only absence flagged
    KILLED  1  M3 shell comments left visible (a commented --env counts as set)
    KILLED  6  M4 cargo-output rule dropped
    KILLED  1  M5 CARGO_TARGET_DIR accepted wherever it points
    KILLED 12  M6 target path built without the separator
    KILLED 10  M7 checkout mount never recognised
    KILLED  9  M8 any bind mount taken for a checkout mount (lab runners judged too)
    KILLED  2  M9 any named volume at all taken as covering the build output
    KILLED  3  M10 image rule dropped (entry point alone would be enough)
    KILLED  7  M11 Dockerfile ENV only in the space form, `ENV X=1` unseen
    KILLED  1  M12 the mount count in the verdict stops being counted
    KILLED 11  M13 findings printed where the caller does not read them
    KILLED  1  M14 an empty root reports success
    KILLED  4  M15 host-side mkdir assumed present
    KILLED  1  M16 any mkdir accepted, container side included
    KILLED  1  M17 mkdir rule stops looking for the target directory
    KILLED  3  M18 mount-point finding names nothing

    mutations: 18  survived: 0
    gate restored byte-identical

M6 is the widest at 12 of the 19 cases: a container path built without the separator makes
every clean fixture report a problem. M7 and M8 are the two directions the mount test can
fail in. M8, treating any bind mount as a checkout mount, is what the lab-runner case exists
to catch, and M7, recognising none, takes the flagging cases down instead -- a check that
finds nothing looks exactly like a clean tree unless something asserts the scan saw the
mount at all, which is what `1 mounts the checkout` in the verdict line is for. M4 covers
both rules that hang off the same loop, which is why it went from 2 kills to 6 when the
mount-point rule joined the build-output rule there. M16 and M17 are the two ways the
`mkdir` test can be too easy: accepting one from anywhere (M16) is what the gate-command
case catches, and dropping the requirement that it names `target` (M17) is what the
host-`mkdir`-of-something-else case catches.

== 9. Wiring

The check runs in CI and in the container entry point, on executable lines in both, and
`check-guard-wiring.py` is what says so rather than a paragraph:

$ python3 scripts/ci/check-guard-wiring.py
    check-guard-wiring: OK (63 files in scripts/ci/, 62 reachable, 58 run by scripts/verify-in-docker.sh, 4 recorded CI-only, 1 exempt)
$ bash scripts/verify-gates.sh --list | grep -n 'hygiene'
    18:container hygiene
$ bash scripts/verify-gates.sh --self-test 2>&1 | tail -1
    verify-gates self-test: 47/47 cases pass

Both new files are among the 58 the entry point runs, so a container run off a clean clone
executes them too.

== 10. The check inside the container

$ IMAGE_TAG=chaos-verify:frozen ./scripts/verify-in-docker.sh --only "container hygiene"
    == source tree: 4014 files, checksum 2672203120
       12 path(s) differ from HEAD, so this run describes the working tree, not a commit
    == --only is in effect: 1 of 34 gates selected, this is not a sweep
    ...
    Ran 19 tests in 0.522s

    OK
    container-hygiene: OK (16 shell script(s) scanned, 1 mounts the checkout, 1 Dockerfile(s), 0 problem(s))

    selected gates passed in chaos-verify:frozen
$ echo "root-owned after: $(find . -path ./target -prune -o -user root -print | wc -l)"
    root-owned after: 0
$ ls -ld target
    drwxrwx--- 8 chaos chaos 4096 Oct  4 13:23 target

The last two lines are the point of the change, measured by the run of the check that guards
it: the gate suite inside the container imports nothing from `scripts/ci` through the mount,
the build directory was already created host-side, and nothing owned by root is left behind.
The `12 path(s) differ from HEAD` line is the runner's own honesty check and describes this
run: the check and its fixtures were not yet committed.

== 11. What this does not cover

The check is static. A mount path assembled at runtime, a `docker run` assembled inside a
variable, or a guard that writes somewhere else on purpose are all invisible to it; catching
those means looking at the tree after a run, which needs Docker, which the gate legs do not
have. It also cannot see the host side: `scripts/verify-gates.sh` runs the same guards as the
developer's own uid, where a cache directory is a cache directory and nothing about ownership
breaks.

The fix does not change which user the container runs as. Running as the host uid would fix
ownership too, but it would also change what the cargo volumes mean (they are root-owned from
years of runs) and what `git` inside the container thinks of `/src`, which the entry point
already works around with `GIT_CONFIG_*`. That trade is a bigger change than the defect.

There is no allow list. If the rule fires on a new entry point, the entry point gets the
`--env` line or stops mounting the checkout; see `scripts/ci/check-script-portability.py` for
why this repository's rules are fixed in the scripts rather than waived in a file.

== 12. Reproduce it

    # the leak, on demand, from the current image: ask for the old behaviour explicitly
    d="$(mktemp -d)" && printf 'X = 1\n' > "$d/probe_mod.py"
    docker run --rm -e PYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE=0 -v "$d:/src" --workdir /src chaos-verify:frozen python3 -c 'import probe_mod'
    ls -ld "$d/__pycache__"          # root root
    rm -rf "$d"; echo "exit=$?"      # Permission denied, 1

    # the fix, same probe without the explicit opt-out
    e="$(mktemp -d)" && printf 'X = 1\n' > "$e/probe_mod.py"
    docker run --rm -v "$e:/src" --workdir /src chaos-verify:frozen python3 -c 'import probe_mod'
    ls -A "$e"; rm -rf "$e"          # only probe_mod.py, and it deletes

    # the check, live and against the pre-fix files
    python3 scripts/ci/check-container-hygiene.py --verbose
    t="$(mktemp -d)" && mkdir -p "$t/scripts" "$t/docker"
    git show "11cdd8a2:scripts/verify-in-docker.sh" > "$t/scripts/verify-in-docker.sh"
    git show "11cdd8a2:docker/verify.Dockerfile" > "$t/docker/verify.Dockerfile"
    python3 scripts/ci/check-container-hygiene.py --root "$t"

    # the mount point, which needs a clone of the pre-fix commit to see
    c="$(mktemp -d)/clone" && git clone -q --no-hardlinks . "$c" && git -C "$c" checkout -q 11cdd8a2
    ( cd "$c" && IMAGE_TAG=chaos-verify:frozen ./scripts/verify-in-docker.sh --only "pipefail report" )
    find "$c" -user root          # ./target, and it is empty
    ( cd "$c" && mkdir -p target && IMAGE_TAG=chaos-verify:frozen ./scripts/verify-in-docker.sh --only "pipefail report" )
    find "$c" -user root | wc -l  # 0

    # the fixtures, and the entry point end to end
    python3 scripts/ci/test-check-container-hygiene.py
    IMAGE_TAG=chaos-verify:frozen ./scripts/verify-in-docker.sh --only "container hygiene"
